<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Local-First Software

The seven ideals from Kleppmann et al. (Ink & Switch, 2019) define the design target.
Keyhive (convergent capabilities + BeeKEM) and Beelay (RIBLT sync + sedimentree) form
the prior-art stack this carrier measures against, the closest to satisfying all seven
simultaneously as read 2026-05-02. The house binds Keyhive behind `KeyhiveProvider`
(`packages/lararium-keyhive`) and runs no Beelay; it syncs through
`@automerge/automerge-repo` over WebSocket.

''Scale ladder (canonical 2026-05-06):''
* ''Lararium'' — one operator's infrastructure: a `lararium-node` process + browser peers + devices.
* ''Nexus'' — a confederation of Lararia sharing a stable internal mesh. Named by community + place.
* ''DreamNet'' — the super-mesh of all Nexuses (allied + oppositional). Within-Nexus = reliable CRDT sync. Cross-Nexus = explicitly brokered, degraded-state-tolerant.

''Core law:'' a `lararium-node` operates as a peer, not an authority — it functions as one Lararium in a Nexus confederation. The server holds no privilege over the content it relays. "The sync server is just another member in the system." (Brooklyn Zelenka, localfirst.fm ep. 19)

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/seven-ideals`>>

!! The Seven Ideals

!!! Ideal 1 — Fast

No server round-trips for reading or writing. Local state always remains available.
UX MUST stay off the network-latency critical path.

''Lararium:'' SATISFIED. TW5Engine runs entirely in-process; tiddler store resides locally;
`filterTiddlers` and `renderTiddler` run synchronously.

''Law:'' Any projection or filter that requires a network call before returning VIOLATES
this ideal. Cache locally; sync asynchronously.

---

!!! Ideal 2 — Multi-Device

Data lives on all devices, not just in a cloud. Sync runs automatically and bidirectionally.
A device holds first-class standing; the cloud serves as an optional relay.

''Lararium:'' SATISFIED between node vessels; a browser device stands built in code
with no e2e witness. A second device mints its OWN key and joins by a signed
device-delegation edge (`lares fleet join` carries the internal `device-admit/v1` edge from `runDeviceAdmitEdge`,
`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/ceremony-core.ts`), and its boot proves that edge at the
Binding Gate (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/boot-daemon-keyhive.ts`). The e2e set carries
the witness: two vessels of one operator sync a bag both ways
(`tests/e2e/meme-two-vessel-bag.test.ts`), and two devices of one PersonaGroup sync
through a herm that reads none of it (`tests/e2e/leaf-sibling-sync.test.ts`). The browser
vessel persists its Repo through `IndexedDBStorageAdapter` and carries its own fleet-join path
(`packages/lararium-browser/src/open-browser-vessel.ts` · `admit-carriage.ts`). The model
stands whole at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/lararium-identity`>>.

''UNBUILT:'' Beelay's RIBLT-based set reconciliation (nested Rateless Invertible Bloom
Lookup Tables), which answers the "what does this peer need?" question without a central
coordinator, stays design (#/beelay-sedimentree).

---

!!! Ideal 3 — Offline

Full read/write capability without a network connection. The system treats offline as the normal mode,
not a degraded fallback.

''Lararium:'' PARTIALLY SATISFIED. TW5 works offline by construction (in-process VM). The
browser vessel persists its Repo to IndexedDB and founds a leaf offline, at a private nexus
of one (`open-browser-vessel.ts`); its genesis materializes from the seed or reloads from
IndexedDB (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-genesis.ts`). The engine and plugin bytes
reach the browser over HTTP into its OPFS store, so a first boot that lacks those bytes needs
a CAS source, and no test witnesses a browser boot with no server present.

''Law:'' The browser peer MUST support booting, reading, and writing from IndexedDB without
any server present. The server functions as a sync accelerator, not a boot requirement.

---

!!! Ideal 4 — Collaboration

Real-time and asynchronous collaboration with conflict-free concurrent edits.
CRDTs provide the enabling mechanism — not operational transforms, not last-write-wins.

''Lararium:'' SATISFIED for shared and cross-operator sync; the concurrent same-meme
witness stays OWED. `AutomergeDocStore` wires each document's change event into
`MemeProvider.handleChange` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/automerge-doc-store.ts` ·
`meme-provider.ts`). Two operators share a realm bag under a charter and a contract, with a
non-member refused (`tests/e2e/meme-realm-bag.test.ts`). No e2e test found here drives two
peers to edit the SAME meme concurrently and asserts the convergence the law below demands;
Automerge supplies that property, and the house owes its own witness of it.

''Law:'' Concurrent edits to the same meme from two peers MUST converge to the same
state on both peers without manual conflict resolution. CRDTs enforce this mechanically.

---

!!! Ideal 5 — Longevity

Software created today remains accessible without the vendor. Data in open formats,
readable independently of the application.

''Lararium:'' SATISFIED. TW5 format — HTML + JS in a single file or tiddler store —
remains readable from 2004 archives. The `text/memetic-wikitext+tiddlywiki` carrier format
consists of plain text with TOML headers; readable by any text editor. For mirrored bags,
`LarDiskProjector` writes memetic carriers as `.mem` files and other carriers in their native
formats (`packages/lararium-node/src/disk-projector.ts`).

''Law:'' No binary-only storage for meme content. Inline meme content rides as UTF-8
text; a pointer carrier holds a content reference in place of `text`. The `cid/` blob tier
carries binary and pointer-kind bodies; a utf8 body rides inline unless an operator flags it
out on purpose
(<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/blob-carriage`>>).

---

!!! Ideal 6 — Privacy

The service provider cannot read user data. End-to-end encryption by default.
Servers hold only encrypted bytes; they see membership info but not document content.

''Lararium:'' PARTIAL. Identity rows rest sealed under the vessel key; document content rests in
cleartext, and the sealed store's later steps seal it
(<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store`>>).

* ''Stands built:'' the custody slot tree and the custody root seal from birth
  (`packages/lararium-node/src/custody-root.ts`). The keyhive archive, each face's veil
  archive and each face's anchors rest sealed under the vessel key from a founding's first
  write, and each row reads whole or refuses by name
  (`packages/lararium-node/src/vault-carriers.ts` · `custody-seat.ts`). Sibling
  devices sync through a herm over a sealed channel the herm cannot read
  (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/leaf-peer-proof.ts`). The sealed-box and ciphertext-CAS
  primitives stand (`sealed-box.ts` · `ciphertext-cas.ts`).
* ''Rests in cleartext:'' Automerge document content in the node store
  (`DurableNodeFSStorageAdapter`); the vessel key file (a 0o600 JSON file,
  `packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts`); the browser's persona
  roots in IndexedDB outside the custody tree, and its device seed and Keyhive Archives
  while no custody slot binds them (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-custody.ts`);
  and vessel-to-vessel sync on the wire, which rides an auth-gated plain WebSocket (this tree
  binds no TLS).
* ''OWED:'' the operator's ruling that every surface encrypts from the first write
  (`lararium-identity#/encrypt-from-start`, a carrier that stands PROPOSED) stays unbuilt
  for document content. The custody rows that rest outside the vessel key, and the order
  the sealed store's steps land in, read at
  <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-road`>>. A WebAuthn-PRF wrap for a browser seed stands as a primitive
  (`packages/lararium-browser/src/seed-wrap-prf.ts`) with no production caller.

''Keyhive target stack (design; BeeKEM content keying stays outside the built path):''

```
Layer 1 — Convergent Capabilities (concap tokens):
  Self-certifying capability chains. "Documents identify themselves via public
  keys and delegate control over themselves to other public keys." Partition-tolerant.

Layer 2 — Group CRDT:
  Coordination-free group membership with revocation. Devices as leaves; users
  as groups of devices. Revocation by blanking tree paths.

Layer 3 — BeeKEM:
  MLS-inspired binary tree key agreement. DH key exchange along leaf-to-root paths.
  Concurrent membership changes via "blanking" and "conflict keys."
  Requires only causal order — no total order needed. (Fuller-consistent.)
```

No non-exportable `SubtleCrypto` key guards a browser seed: each rests as listed above.

---

!!! Ideal 7 — User Control

Users can move, copy, inspect, and modify their data. No lock-in. The application serves as
a tool, not a custodian.

''Lararium:'' SATISFIED for single-user. The `lar:` URI namespace forms an open addressing
scheme; the tiddler store admits inspection and export.

''Multi-user:'' capability gating stands. A realm bag admits a member and refuses a
non-member (`tests/e2e/meme-realm-bag.test.ts`); Orichalcum supplies the vocabulary
(<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/orichalcum-capabilities`>>) and the residency
ACTION verbs carry the acts (`ACTION_VERBS`, `packages/lararium-mesh/src/residency-actions.ts`).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/server-as-peer`>>

!! Server-as-Peer Law

A lararium-node operates as a peer with extra capabilities:
* Acts as multiple peers simultaneously (one per open wiki island)
* Disk access (LarDiskProjector)
* CORS-hop web calls
* Persistent relay (stays online when browser peers sleep)

It does NOT have:
* Special authority over content truth
* Ability to decrypt content it relays (Ideal 6 target)
* Override capability for user-controlled data

As built, a herm cannot decrypt the sibling-channel content it carries, while a hearth still
relays cleartext document sync; the second line states the Ideal 6 target, owed there.

''Law:'' A lararium-node MUST keep its decisions within its peer role.
Routing, relay, and projection count as peer capabilities; adjudication does not.

''Readiness reads local (corollary, 2026-06-10):'' a vessel declares its own
readiness from local state (the server listens; the port stands open) — it MUST
NOT gate readiness on a peer's arrival, which imports a global-now dependency
and deadlocks every storage-miss resolve while the vessel sits peerless.
(Witnessed: upstream's WS //server// adapter waits for a first connection;
its //client// adapter force-readies on a timer — the vessel corrects the server
side with `ListeningWSServerAdapter`.)

One composition enforces this isomorphically: the browser vessel and the node vessel both
compose through `composeCoreVessel` (`composeBrowser` · `composeLararium`), the one keel
both substrates walk. Same model, different capability set.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/fuller-universe`>>

!! Fuller's Non-Simultaneous Universe = Causal Island Doctrine

Fuller (Synergetics §301): //"the aggregate of all humanity's consciously apprehended//
//and communicated nonsimultaneous and only partially overlapping experiences."//

''Universe has no global now.'' Events have finite durations; they partially overlap;
no single simultaneous snapshot of the whole coheres or meets any need.

This supplies the theoretical grounding for causal island doctrine:
* The lararium mesh has no global clock, no global state.
* Each island has a causal history (partially-ordered event log).
* Islands overlap "only partially" — they share some events (via sync), not all.

''Operational consequence:''
Sync protocols requiring total order (a single source of truth for "current state")
violate Fuller's Universe model. CRDT-based sync (Automerge + Beelay) requires
only ''causal order'' — Fuller-consistent by construction. BeeKEM's
"only a causal order of operations" requirement gives the cryptographic expression
of Fuller's non-simultaneous Universe.

''The UI MUST reflect this:'' content reads "current as of my last sync with peer X,"
not "current globally." A lararium-node MUST present its view as local — current as of last sync.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/beelay-sedimentree`>>

!! Beelay Sync Model

''UNBUILT in this house: design only.'' Beelay provides a sync protocol for Automerge repos.
Key mechanisms:

''RIBLT (Rateless Invertible Bloom Lookup Tables):''
Nested set reconciliation at three levels — membership operations, document states,
per-document CGKA operations. Peers exchange RIBLT digests; the difference forms the
sync payload. No central coordinator; no round-trip to determine "what do you need?"

''Sedimentree:''
The commit DAG compressed with geological layering: older chunks compressed more
aggressively ("deeper = older = denser"). Solves the long-document history problem.
A fresh peer can sync efficiently even against a document with years of history.

''Lararium integration point:'' `AutomergeDocStore` wires the `DocHandle.on("change", ...)`
event into `MemeProvider.handleChange()`. Any sync protocol operates below this boundary —
transparent to the meme layer — so Beelay would slot in beneath it, where
`@automerge/automerge-repo`'s own sync runs.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/alignment-matrix`>>

!! Alignment Matrix (measured 2026-10-12)

| Ideal | State | What stays owed |
|---|---|---|
| Fast | ✓ SATISFIED | — |
| Multi-device | ✓ SATISFIED (node) | an e2e witness for a browser device |
| Offline | ⚠ PARTIAL | a browser first boot with no server |
| Collaboration | ✓ SATISFIED | a concurrent same-meme convergence witness |
| Longevity | ✓ SATISFIED | — |
| Privacy | ⚠ PARTIAL | document content sealed at rest and on the wire; the custody train |
| User control | ✓ SATISFIED | — |

